import{_ as t,o as s,c as a,ag as n}from"./chunks/framework.2cFhd8Ux.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Agent Patterns","description":"","frontmatter":{"source":"resources/agent-patterns.md"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"resources/agent-patterns.md","filePath":"resources/agent-patterns.md"}'),o={name:"resources/agent-patterns.md"};function r(i,e,h,l,d,p){return s(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n(`<h1 id="agent-patterns" tabindex="-1">Agent Patterns <a class="header-anchor" href="#agent-patterns" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Agent Patterns&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Context-engineering patterns for the <em>Delegate</em> step: how to set an agent up so that what it does is small, checkable and yours. Each pattern says when to use it, how, the evidence, and how it fails.</p></blockquote><p>The patterns are tool-agnostic: every current harness (Gemini CLI, Copilot, OpenCode, Claude Code, your own <code>mini-agent</code>) supports them in some form. Commands and menus differ; <a href="./../TOOLS.html">Tool Handout</a> says which tool is current. Where the evidence for a pattern is practitioner consensus rather than a study, the pattern says so.</p><p><strong>On this page:</strong> <a href="#agentsmd-structure">AGENTS.md structure</a> · <a href="#plan-first">Plan first</a> · <a href="#small-tasks">Small tasks</a> · <a href="#check-first">Check first</a> · <a href="#subagents">Subagents</a> · <a href="#worktrees">Worktrees</a> · <a href="#approval-policies">Approval policies</a> · <a href="#the-goldfish-test">The Goldfish test</a> · <a href="#measuring-whether-the-agent-follows-it">Measuring whether the agent follows it</a></p><h2 id="agentsmd-structure" tabindex="-1">AGENTS.md structure <a class="header-anchor" href="#agentsmd-structure" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;AGENTS.md structure&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>What.</strong> A short file at the repository root that every session reads first. It tells the agent what it can&#39;t find out quickly by itself: the exact commands, where things live, the conventions it would otherwise get wrong, and the boundaries.</p><p><strong>How.</strong> Use <a href="./../templates/AGENTS.html">AGENTS.md template</a>. The sections, in order:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Holds</th><th>Keep it to</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Project</td><td>One paragraph: what this is, which part your team owns</td><td>3 lines</td></tr><tr><td>Commands</td><td>Exact build, test, lint and single-test commands, as copied from a working terminal</td><td>5–8 lines</td></tr><tr><td>Map</td><td>Entry points and the directories that matter, with one phrase each</td><td>5–10 lines</td></tr><tr><td>Conventions</td><td>Only the non-obvious ones, especially those an agent got wrong before</td><td>5–10 lines</td></tr><tr><td>Workflow</td><td>Plan first; tests first; one task per session; the evidence to show when done</td><td>5 lines</td></tr><tr><td>Scope</td><td>Directories it may edit; what it must ask about first</td><td>5 lines</td></tr><tr><td>Never</td><td>Destructive commands, secrets, weakening tests, posting anything</td><td>5–8 lines</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Evidence.</strong> In 441 repositories, coding-agent adoption raised commits 28–38%; agent-first repositories <strong>without</strong> a committed configuration file saw code complexity rise 53%, against 27% for those with one. The study is observational, and <strong>73.8% of configuration files were never edited after their first commit</strong> (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.25241" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Denisov-Blanch et al.</a>). Agent PRs that get merged often need follow-up for project conventions (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.14745" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Watanabe et al.</a>), which is what the Conventions section is for. Every line is read in every session, and context degrades with length (<a href="./glossary.html#context-rot">context rot</a>).</p><p><strong>Fails when.</strong> It becomes a copy of the README (long, generic, ignored); it is never updated (add a line the second time an agent makes the same mistake, and date the file); or you treat it as enforcement. In two famous incidents agents broke written rules they could recite afterwards (<a href="./case-studies.html#c2-pocketos-a-token-found-in-an-unrelated-file">case C2</a>). Rules go in <code>AGENTS.md</code>; guarantees go in permissions, tokens and the sandbox. And an <code>AGENTS.md</code> you didn&#39;t write is a stranger&#39;s instructions to your agent (<a href="./case-studies.html#a9-the-rules-file-backdoor">case A9</a>): read it before your agent does.</p><h2 id="plan-first" tabindex="-1">Plan first <a class="header-anchor" href="#plan-first" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Plan first&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>What.</strong> The agent reads and proposes; it edits nothing until you approve a plan.</p><p><strong>How.</strong> Use the harness&#39;s plan mode, or say so:</p><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>Don&#39;t edit anything yet. Read what you need, then propose a plan:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>the files you will change, the steps in order, the test that will show each step works,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>and anything you&#39;re unsure about. Wait for my approval.</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Then edit the plan: delete steps that touch files outside the task, reorder so the test comes first, and ask about anything you don&#39;t understand. Approve one step at a time.</p><p><strong>Evidence.</strong> Practitioner consensus and course experience rather than a controlled study. Michigan&#39;s EECS 498 requires &quot;Learn, then design, then build&quot;, because &quot;A one-shot prompt gives you the model&#39;s design, not yours&quot; (<a href="https://github.com/eecs498-aase/materials/blob/HEAD/labs/Lab03/slides.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Lab03</a>).</p><p><strong>Fails when.</strong> You approve a plan you skimmed. A plan is a design review in miniature: if you couldn&#39;t defend it to a teammate, don&#39;t approve it.</p><h2 id="small-tasks" tabindex="-1">Small tasks <a class="header-anchor" href="#small-tasks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Small tasks&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>What.</strong> One task = one session = one diff a teammate can review in one sitting.</p><p><strong>How.</strong> Split work in <code>TASKS.md</code> until each task names its files, its test and its &quot;done when&quot;. Start a fresh session per task rather than continuing a long one. If a task needs a design decision, it isn&#39;t an agent task yet.</p><p><strong>Evidence.</strong> Agents&#39; reliability falls with task length: METR&#39;s time-horizon measure is the task length an agent completes with 50% reliability, and longer tasks fail more (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.14499" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Kwa et al.</a>). Performance falls as context grows in every model Chroma tested, and a focused 300-token prompt beat a 113K-token one (<a href="https://www.trychroma.com/research/context-rot" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Hong et al.</a>). Compaction keeps long sessions going by summarizing, which loses detail.</p><p><strong>Fails when.</strong> &quot;Small&quot; is measured in prompts rather than in the diff. A one-line prompt can produce a 900-line change.</p><h2 id="check-first" tabindex="-1">Check first <a class="header-anchor" href="#check-first" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Check first&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>What.</strong> The test that defines &quot;done&quot; exists, and fails, before the agent writes the code.</p><p><strong>How.</strong> Write the test yourself, or have a <strong>separate session</strong> write it from the acceptance criterion and the function&#39;s signature only, never from the implementation. Run it and see it fail. Put &quot;never weaken, skip or delete a test&quot; in <code>AGENTS.md</code>&#39;s Never section, and check the diff for it anyway. For logic with a clear invariant, write a property test (week 6); measure the suite with mutation testing (week 7).</p><p><strong>Evidence.</strong> Tests generated after faulty code caught 14% of its faults against 25% for independently generated tests (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.05139" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Konstantinou et al.</a>); LLM-generated oracles capture actual rather than expected behavior (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.21136" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Konstantinou, Degiovanni &amp; Papadakis</a>); LLM-written tests rarely probe boundaries (null inputs 34.8%, empty 38.8%, against over 93% for booleans: <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32548" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Shi et al.</a>).</p><p><strong>Fails when.</strong> The agent sees the test and writes code that special-cases it. Review the implementation, not just the green check.</p><h2 id="subagents" tabindex="-1">Subagents <a class="header-anchor" href="#subagents" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Subagents&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>What.</strong> A parent agent starts a child session with a fresh context for a sub-task (search the codebase, summarize a module, review a diff) and receives only the child&#39;s summary.</p><p><strong>When.</strong> Exploration of a large codebase whose raw output would flood the parent&#39;s context; independent reads that can run in parallel; a review by a session that didn&#39;t write the code.</p><p><strong>How.</strong> Give the subagent a narrow question and a required output shape (&quot;list each file that calls <code>createBooking</code>, with the line number&quot;). Treat what comes back as <strong>inferred</strong> until you check a sample of it.</p><p><strong>Evidence.</strong> Thin. Subagents are taught as a harness technique (Michigan EECS 498&#39;s later lectures; ANU&#39;s COMP4020 &quot;Subagents and real-time&quot;: <a href="https://eecs498-aase.github.io/syllabus.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Michigan</a>, <a href="https://comp.anu.edu.au/courses/comp4020-agentic-coding-studio/lectures/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">ANU</a>), but no study found by the course measures their effect on quality.</p><p><strong>Fails when.</strong> A subagent&#39;s confident summary becomes a fact in your onboarding report without anyone opening the files. And each subagent spends from the same allowance.</p><h2 id="worktrees" tabindex="-1">Worktrees <a class="header-anchor" href="#worktrees" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Worktrees&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>What.</strong> Separate working directories on separate branches of one repository, so several agents can work at once without touching each other&#39;s files.</p><p><strong>How.</strong></p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">git</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> worktree</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> add</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> ../roomly-issue-3</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -b</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> issue-3</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">git</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> worktree</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> add</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> ../roomly-issue-5</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -b</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> issue-5</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">git</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> worktree</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> list</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Start one agent in each directory. Each worktree needs its own <code>npm install</code> and, if it runs a server, its own port. When a branch is merged:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">git</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> worktree</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> remove</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> ../roomly-issue-3</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">git</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> worktree</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> prune</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Before you start, write down each task&#39;s <strong>merge point</strong>: where the branches will meet, and who resolves conflicts there.</p><p><strong>Evidence.</strong> Topics and demos only; no course has published outcome data. UT Austin teaches &quot;Parallelize a task once you can name its merge point&quot; (<a href="https://www.cs.utexas.edu/~witchel/378AC/schedule.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">schedule</a>); UMD&#39;s CMSC 389A lists &quot;concurrent agents, git worktrees&quot; as an outcome (<a href="https://www.cs.umd.edu/class/fall2026/cmsc389A/Syllabus.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">syllabus</a>). Week 13 asks you to measure your own review time, because review, not generation, is the likely bottleneck.</p><p><strong>Fails when.</strong> Two tasks touch the same module (a conflict you scheduled), or three agents&#39; output arrives faster than you can review it. Problems: <a href="./troubleshooting.html#git-worktrees">troubleshooting</a>.</p><h2 id="approval-policies" tabindex="-1">Approval policies <a class="header-anchor" href="#approval-policies" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Approval policies&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>What.</strong> The rules for which tool calls run without asking. The course default is <strong>ask for every command and every write</strong> (<a href="./../setup/safety-contract.html#2-approvals-stay-on">rule 2</a>).</p><p><strong>How.</strong> If you allowlist, allowlist read-only commands whose effect you can predict: <code>git status</code>, <code>git diff</code>, <code>ls</code>. Never allowlist anything that runs arbitrary code: a shell (<code>bash -c</code>), an interpreter (<code>node</code>, <code>python</code>), <code>npx</code>, or a package install. Note that <code>npm test</code> and <code>node --test</code> run whatever the tests contain, including code the agent just wrote, so allowlist them only in a sandbox with no secrets attached. <strong>Check how your harness matches.</strong> <code>mini-agent</code>&#39;s allowlist matches prefixes, so <code>npm test &amp;&amp; curl …</code> passes a rule for <code>npm test</code>; week 1&#39;s &quot;smarter approval policy&quot; extension is about exactly that, and about the limits of parsing a shell string. An allowlist beats a deny-list, because a deny-list has to anticipate every dangerous spelling. Approve writes by reading the diff, not the agent&#39;s description of it. Treat any change to an agent&#39;s own config or permission files as a security change.</p><p><strong>Evidence.</strong> Injected text has made an agent switch itself to auto-approve (<a href="./case-studies.html#a4-copilot-edits-its-own-permissions-and-camoleak">case A4</a>); in an autopilot mode, a web page exfiltrated secrets in about 28 seconds (<a href="./case-studies.html#a7-autopilot-a-web-page-that-sent-secrets-away">case A7</a>). Approval fatigue is real: Anthropic reports that sandboxing cut its internal permission prompts by 84% (<a href="https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/claude-code-sandboxing" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Anthropic</a>), the right way to reduce prompts.</p><p><strong>Fails when.</strong> You approve by reflex. If you&#39;re approving more than you&#39;re reading, the task is too big or the sandbox is too loose.</p><h2 id="the-goldfish-test" tabindex="-1">The Goldfish test <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-goldfish-test" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The Goldfish test&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>What.</strong> Hand a <strong>fresh</strong> session (no memory of your work) only the document, and see what it does. A goldfish remembers nothing; if it can&#39;t work from the document alone, the document is leaning on context that lives in your head. From Michigan EECS 498&#39;s Elephant-Goldfish Model, which requires an &quot;Elephant design document and Goldfish review ledger&quot; before implementation (<a href="https://eecs498-aase.github.io/syllabus.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">syllabus</a>; <a href="https://github.com/eecs498-aase/materials/blob/HEAD/egm/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">EGM</a>).</p><p><strong>How, for a specification:</strong></p><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>You have only the document below, and no other context about this project.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>1. In five sentences, say what you would build.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>2. List every question you would need answered before starting.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>3. List every assumption you would have to make to start without asking.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Do not write any code.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>[paste SPEC.md, or TASKS.md]</span></span></code></pre></div><p><strong>How, for <code>AGENTS.md</code>:</strong> start a new session in the repository, give it a small real task, and watch whether it runs the right commands, stays in scope and follows the workflow without your help.</p><p>Every question and wrong assumption is a defect in the document. Fix the document, not the session, and record the run in <code>AI_LOG.md</code>.</p><p><strong>Fails when.</strong> You run it in the session where you wrote the document (it remembers), or you answer its questions in chat instead of in the document.</p><h2 id="measuring-whether-the-agent-follows-it" tabindex="-1">Measuring whether the agent follows it <a class="header-anchor" href="#measuring-whether-the-agent-follows-it" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Measuring whether the agent follows it&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Week 3 asks you to test your <code>AGENTS.md</code>, not just write it.</p><ol><li>Pick two real tasks and, for each, two or three rules you can check in the transcript or the diff (&quot;ran the single-test command before finishing&quot;, &quot;edited nothing outside <code>src/</code>&quot;).</li><li>Run each task <strong>with</strong> and <strong>without</strong> the file (or with two versions of it), same model, same starting commit, in a fresh session each time. Models are nondeterministic: run each condition at least three times.</li><li>Record what you <strong>observed</strong> per run: rule followed, partly, or not, with the evidence.</li><li>Report counts, not adjectives, and scope the claim to the sample. Stanford CS146S&#39;s assignment puts it well: &quot;Three repos is a small sample. Scope your claims to what it supports&quot; (<a href="https://github.com/mihail911/modern-software-dev-assignments/blob/df18839a81/week5/assignment.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">W5 spec</a>).</li></ol><p>Template for the results: <a href="./../templates/AI_LOG.html#experiments">AI_LOG.md template: Experiments</a>.</p>`,58)])])}const g=t(o,[["render",r]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
